.\" zshrs.1 — man page for zshrs(1)
.\" Companion: zshrsall(1) — comprehensive reference covering the
.\" daemon, AOT compiler, fusevm bytecode runtime, AOP intercepts,
.\" parity modes, caches, and recorder.
.TH ZSHRS 1 "2026-10-18" "zshrs 0.13.55" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zshrs \- the first compiled Unix shell (drop-in zsh replacement)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zshrs
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ argument
\&...]
.PP
.B zshrs
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument
\&...]
.PP
.B zshrs
.B \-s
.RI [ argument
\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zshrs
is a drop-in replacement for the Z shell
.RB ( zsh (1)),
rewritten in Rust. It is the first Unix shell to compile to bytecode
and execute on a purpose-built virtual machine
.RB ( fusevm )
rather than walk an AST at run time. Source compatibility with zsh is
maintained at the lexer, parser, builtin, parameter, glob, and ZLE
layers; existing
.IR .zshrc ,
.IR .zprofile ,
zinit/oh-my-zsh plugins, and the
.B zsh-more-completions
corpus run unchanged.
.PP
Beyond compatibility,
.B zshrs
adds: a persistent worker pool that eliminates most
.BR fork (2)
overhead for builtins; aspect-oriented programming
.RB ( AOP )
intercepts on every command; rkyv-backed bytecode image caches for
scripts, plugins, and autoload functions; an optional
singleton coordinator daemon
.RB ( zshrs-daemon ,
see
.BR zd (1));
and ahead-of-time compilation
.RB ( AOT )
through the
.B zbuild
builtin, which turns scripts into a single executable.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Special options
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the zshrs version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-emulate " MODE"
zsh's own option: run as zsh with a full
.B emulate
.I MODE
installed before any later option word is read. It must be the first
option word.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Full diagnostic report of shell health, cache state, daemon presence,
worker pool, and integrity scan results.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the same report as one JSON object.
.TP
.B \-\-banner
Print the ZSHRS logo, a box with the version and the builtin and
extension totals, and whether the daemon answers on its socket, then exit.
Inside a shell the
.B zbanner
builtin prints the same, plus that shell's function, alias, parameter and
job counts.
.TP
.BI \-\-prewarm\-autoloads " [DIR ...]"
Compile every
.B _*
completion function on
.I $fpath
(or on the named directories) into
.IR ~/.zshrs/autoloads.rkyv ,
then print a one-line JSON summary and exit. A later autoload of any of
those functions installs it straight from the cached bytecode instead of
reading and parsing its definition file, so the first
.B "ls \-<TAB>"
of a fresh shell costs an index lookup rather than a parse. Entries whose
definition file still matches the recorded mtime and length are skipped.
.IP
Runs in its own process on purpose: the parser walks process-global lexer
state and must not run beside a live line editor. The same pass runs at
the end of every
.BR zshrs\-recorder (1)
run, and is reachable through the daemon as
.BR "zd prewarm" .
Inside a shell the
.B prewarm\-autoloads
builtin runs the same pass, on the shell's own
.I $fpath
when no directory is named.
.SS Parity modes
Each parity mode disables all caches and the daemon and matches the
named reference shell byte-for-byte; every
.B source
re-runs the file fresh, every
.B echo
re-fires.
.TP
.B \-\-zsh
Drop-in for
.BR /bin/zsh .
Entry point for compatibility tests.
Behaviour upstream changed after zsh 5.9.2 follows the development tree,
except when the binary is started as
.B zsh
.RI ( argv[0] ),
where the 5.9.2 answer is kept: for example
.B let
after a math error returns 1 there and 2 otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-bash
Drop-in for
.BR /bin/bash .
.TP
.B \-\-ksh
Drop-in for
.BR /bin/ksh
(ksh-93 semantics).
.TP
.BR \-\-posix ", " \-\-sh
Drop-in for
.BR /bin/sh
(Bourne / dash).
.TP
.BR \-\-dash ", " \-\-ash
Drop-in for
.BR dash (1)
and its
.BR ash (1)
ancestor.
.TP
.BR \-\-mksh ", " \-\-pdksh
Drop-in for
.BR mksh (1)
and
.BR pdksh (1),
which differ from ksh-93 in option set and arithmetic edge cases.
.TP
.B \-\-csh
Drop-in for
.BR csh (1).
Outside the Bourne family; provided for argv[0] dispatch.
.TP
.B \-\-zsh\-compat
Legacy alias for
.BR \-\-zsh .
.PP
The parity matrix (\fBtests/emulation_parity.rs\fR) compares the standard output and exit status of
.B zshrs \-\-X
with the real shell X over shared and per\-dialect script corpora, generated operator grids and tagged data files (every script runs in an empty scratch directory with a 30 second limit, and the ways run in parallel), and the two zsh\-style legs with
.B zsh
running
.B "emulate sh"
and
.BR "emulate ksh" .
It prefers the newest installed reference (bash 5.3 and ksh93u+m, not macOS's bash 3.2 and
ksh93u+ 2012), because those are the versions zshrs reports. Differences it pins include:
.BR $OPTARG ,
.BR $UID ,
.BR $EUID ,
.BR $OSTYPE ,
.BR $COLUMNS " and " $!
being unset where the real shell leaves them unset;
.B $10
meaning
.B ${1}0
under sh and ksh;
.B break
outside a loop being a no\-op; dash's
.BR "set \-o"
names, \fBecho\fR and \fBprintf\fR escapes; bash's
.BR "export \-n" ,
.BR "cd ''" ,
.BR "exit abc" ;
mksh's missing
.B **
and
.BR {1..3} ;
ksh93's missing
.BR local ;
and each shell's usage\-error status for
.BR kill ,
.BR ulimit ,
.BR fg ,
.BR umask " and " cd .
.SS bash startup-file options
Accepted in
.B \-\-bash
mode only; outside it they are rejected the way
.BR zsh (1)
rejects an unknown option.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
Do not read
.I ~/.bashrc
for an interactive non-login shell.
.TP
.B \-\-noprofile
Do not read
.I /etc/profile
or the
.I ~/.bash_profile
chain for a login shell.
.TP
.BI \-\-rcfile " FILE"
Read
.I FILE
instead of
.IR ~/.bashrc .
The separate-word form only, as bash requires.
.TP
.BI \-\-init\-file " FILE"
Alias of
.BR \-\-rcfile .
.PP
Two notions of fidelity are available, and they are not the same shell.
.B \-\-sh
reproduces
.BR /bin/sh ;
.B "\-\-sh \-\-zsh"
reproduces
.IR "zsh's model of" " /bin/sh" .
Concretely, the bare POSIX modes use XSI
.BR echo ,
where zsh's
.B "emulate sh"
sets
.BR BSD_ECHO ;
and
.B \-\-bash
accepts bash's own
.B "set \-o"
names, including the six zsh has no option for.
.PP
With no parity flag, zshrs runs in full mode: rkyv
.IR script_cache " + " plugin_cache " + " daemon
enabled.
.SS Editor and developer tooling
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Serve the Language Server Protocol over stdin/stdout: completion, hover,
definition, references, documentHighlight, documentSymbol, foldingRange,
rename, semanticTokens, formatting and publishDiagnostics. Completion
answers from the shell's own compsys completers, so
.B "git ch"
completes
.B checkout
inside the editor. Framing and JSON-RPC are hand-rolled; no LSP crate is
linked in. Set
.B ZSHRS_LSP_LOG
to a path to dump every request and response.
.TP
.BI \-\-dap " [HOST:PORT]"
Serve the Debug Adapter Protocol \(em breakpoints, stepping, frames,
scopes, variables and evaluate \(em so any DAP client can debug a zsh
script. With a
.I HOST:PORT
argument the adapter connects back to the client's listener; with no
argument (or when the next word is not an address) it serves over stdio.
.TP
.BI \-\-docs " [TOPIC | PAGE | \-t | \-l | \-s " PAT ]
With no argument, open the reference book: zshrs's manual and guides, its
builtins, and the whole zsh manual \(em every builtin, option, parameter,
expansion, ZLE widget, completion style and module \(em paged in the
terminal, one part per manual page and one chapter per section. Every
screen carries the ZSHRS logo, a status box naming the page number, the
topics on the page and their chapter, and a key line over a
.B >>>
prompt; the table of contents, search results and key list scroll with
.B j
/
.BR k .
.B j
/
.B k
turn pages,
.B ]
/
.B [
change chapter,
.B }
/
.B {
change part,
.B t
shows the contents,
.B /
searches,
.B :
jumps to a page,
.B ?
lists every key and
.B q
quits. With a
.IR TOPIC ,
print that entry: the LSP hover card (styled on a terminal, markdown
otherwise), or, for a name only the manual documents \(em a widget, a
completion style, an expansion flag \(em its manual entry. With a
.IR PAGE ,
.BR \-t " (contents), " \-l " (every topic) or " \-s " PAT (search),"
print and exit. Without a terminal on stdin and stdout, or with
.B ZSHRS_NO_TTY
set, the book prints its first page instead of waiting for keys.
.TP
.BI \-\-fmt " [\-w] [\-t] [\-i " N ] " [FILE ...]"
Format zsh source: block-structure reindent and idiomatic spacing per the
zsh and Oh-My-Zsh style guides, heredoc bodies left verbatim, idempotent.
With no
.I FILE
it reads stdin and writes stdout;
.B \-w
rewrites each file in place and requires file arguments;
.B \-t
indents with tabs;
.BI \-i " N"
sets the indent width (default 4). The same engine backs the LSP's
formatting capability, so editor and command line cannot drift.
.TP
.BI \-\-ztest " [\-j " N ] " [\-q] [PATH ...]"
Run the builtin unit-test framework over the named paths. Tests execute on
a worker pool of persistent subprocesses \(em one per CPU by default,
.BI \-j " N"
to override \(em which fork per test and report over a JSON pipe, with
per-test capture of file descriptor 2 so concurrent workers cannot tear
each other's output.
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
suppress per-test lines. The assertions themselves
(the assertion verbs \(em
.BR zassert_eq ", " zassert_ne ", " zassert_ok ", " zassert_match
and the rest \(em plus
.BR ztest_skip " and " ztest_run )
are shell builtins, callable from any script with nothing installed.
.TP
.B \-\-names
List every name
.B \-\-docs
knows \(em builtins, keywords, options, special parameters, compsys
functions and extensions \(em one per line, which is what drives
.B "zshrs \-\-docs <TAB>" .
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-reflection
Emit a JSON self-description of every builtin, keyword and option, for
tools that would otherwise scrape
.BR \-\-help .
.B \-\-dump\-reference\-html
emits the same corpus as HTML chapter sections, one
.B <article>
per name.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-plugins
Emit JSON for every sourced plugin, grouped by the manager that installed
it (zinit, oh\-my\-zsh, prezto, antidote, antigen, zplug, or loose). The
JetBrains plugin turns each entry into an IDE library root, so plugin
functions are navigable and renameable across plugin boundaries.
.TP
.BI \-\-gen\-docs " [PATH] [\-\-out DIR]"
Walk
.I PATH
(default the current directory) for shell sources (.zsh, .sh, .bash, .ksh and rc dotfiles)
and write Markdown module docs for each under
.I DIR
(default
.IR docs/ ),
mirroring the source layout. A contiguous block of
.B ##
lines immediately above a function declaration documents that function; a
.B ##
block at the top of a file is the module header.
.TP
.BI \-\-tiers " FILE"
Run the script, then report which fusevm execution tier took each of its
chunks, asked of fusevm's own eligibility and cache predicates.
.TP
.BI \-\-csh\-translate " [\-\-source] FILE"
Print the zsh translation of a csh script (\fB\-\fR reads stdin).
.B \-\-source
translates text a csh
.B source
runs inside a function, so its
.B exit
leaves only the sourced file.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-tokens " FILE"
Print one TOKNAME<tab>TOKSTR line per lexer token.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-ast " FILE"
Print the parser AST as a canonical S-expression.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-wordcode " FILE"
Print the wordcode emitter output.
.IP
For these three,
.I FILE
may be
.B \-
for stdin; the output format matches the C-side
.B zshrs_dump
module's dumpers, so it can be diffed against
.BR zsh (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-zwc " ZWCFILE [FUNCTION]"
List the functions in a compiled
.I .zwc
file, or dump one of them.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the fusevm opcodes of each compiled unit before the VM runs it;
execution continues.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " always|never"
With
.BR \-\-docs ,
force ANSI colour on or off. Without it, colour follows
.BR NO_COLOR ,
.BR FORCE_COLOR ,
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
and whether stdout is a terminal.
.SS Standard zsh single-letter options
The following match
.BR zsh (1)
exactly. Long-option names follow each.
.TP
.B \-b
End option processing
.RB ( -- ).
.TP
.B \-c
Take the first argument as a command and execute it.
.TP
.B \-f
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-no\-rcs
(skip startup files).
.TP
.B \-i
Force interactive mode.
.TP
.B \-l
.RB ( \-\-login )
force login shell.
.TP
.B \-s
Read commands from stdin.
.TP
.BI \-o " option"
Set
.I option
by name; see
.BR zshoptions (1).
.TP
.B \-v
.RB ( \-\-verbose )
echo each command line after history substitution.
.TP
.B \-x
.RB ( \-\-xtrace )
echo each command and its arguments as they are executed.
.PP
A complete table is printed by
.BR "zshrs \-\-help" .
The single-letter set tracks
.BR zsh (1)
literally:
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-y
and the upper-case
.BR \-H " " \-I " " \-J " " \-K " " \-L " " \-M " " \-N " " \-O " " \-P " " \-Q " " \-R " " \-S " " \-T " " \-U " " \-V " " \-W " " \-X " " \-Y " " \-Z
family.
.SH STARTUP FILES
In its native mode and under
.BR \-\-zsh ,
zshrs reads the same startup files as
.BR zsh (1)
in the same order:
.IP "" 4
.I /etc/zshenv
.IP "" 4
.IB $ZDOTDIR /.zshenv
.RB ( $HOME
when
.B $ZDOTDIR
is unset)
.IP "" 4
.I /etc/zprofile
,
.IB $ZDOTDIR /.zprofile
(login)
.IP "" 4
.I /etc/zshrc
,
.IB $ZDOTDIR /.zshrc
(interactive)
.IP "" 4
.I /etc/zlogin
,
.IB $ZDOTDIR /.zlogin
(login)
.IP "" 4
.IB $ZDOTDIR /.zlogout
,
.I /etc/zlogout
(login shutdown)
.PP
.B \-\-no\-rcs
.RB ( \-f )
and the
.B PRIVILEGED
option modulate which files are sourced, identically to zsh.
.SS Use as a login shell
.B chsh
takes a path, not a flag, so a drop-in is selected the way
.BR login (1)
and
.BR sshd
start a shell: symlink the binary under the target shell's name and let it
be exec'd with a leading dash on
.IR argv[0] .
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ln -s /path/to/zshrs ~/bin/bash
# add ~/bin/bash to /etc/shells, then:
chsh -s ~/bin/bash
.EE
.RE
.PP
The leading dash is the only signal a login shell gets: it selects the
personality, sets
.B shopt login_shell
and drives the profile chain and
.IR ~/.bash_logout .
In
.B \-\-bash
mode the bash
.B $PS1
backslash escapes
.RB ( \\u ", " \\h ", " \\w ", " \\W ", " \\$ ", " \\t ", " \\A ", " \\d ,
.BR \\D{ fmt } ", " \\j ", " \\[ / \\] ,
octal) are expanded as bash expands them, and
.B ${var@P}
reports the expansion.
.SS Emulation drop-ins
Each drop-in reads the startup files of the shell it stands in for, not
zsh's.
.B \-\-no\-rcs
suppresses all of them.
.TP
.B \-\-bash
Login:
.I /etc/profile
then the first readable of
.IR ~/.bash_profile ", " ~/.bash_login ", " ~/.profile .
Interactive non-login:
.I /etc/bash.bashrc
(where the platform ships one) then
.IR ~/.bashrc .
Non-interactive:
.BR $BASH_ENV .
A login shell reads
.I ~/.bash_logout
on exit.
.TP
.B \-\-ksh
Login:
.I /etc/profile
then
.IR ~/.profile .
Interactive:
.I /etc/ksh.kshrc
(where present) then
.BR $ENV ,
which defaults to
.IR ~/.kshrc .
.TP
.B \-\-mksh
As
.BR \-\-ksh ,
but
.B $ENV
falls back to
.I ~/.mkshrc
rather than
.IR ~/.kshrc .
.TP
.B \-\-pdksh
As
.BR \-\-ksh ,
but
.B $ENV
has no default.
.TP
.BR \-\-sh ", " \-\-posix ", " \-\-dash ", " \-\-ash
Login:
.I /etc/profile
then
.IR ~/.profile .
Interactive:
.B $ENV
with no default.
.TP
.B \-\-csh
Always:
.I /etc/csh.cshrc
then
.IR ~/.tcshrc ,
or
.I ~/.cshrc
when
.I ~/.tcshrc
is absent. A login shell additionally reads
.I /etc/csh.login
and
.IR ~/.login ,
and reads
.I ~/.logout
and
.I /etc/csh.logout
on exit.
.PP
An interactive LOGIN shell reads both its profile and its interactive
file in every mode except
.BR \-\-bash ,
which reads the profile only. A PRIVILEGED shell reads no user file; the
Korn and Bourne drop-ins take
.I /etc/suid_profile
in place of the profile pair.
.PP
Rules that differ per shell, each measured against the reference binary
rather than assumed:
.TP
.B Login by argv[0] alone
A non-interactive shell that is a login shell only because of the dash on
.I argv[0]
reads the profile in every mode EXCEPT
.BR \-\-bash ,
which additionally requires the shell to be interactive or
.B \-l
to have been given explicitly.
.TP
.B \-f
means
.B noglob
under
.BR \-\-bash ", " \-\-ksh ", " \-\-mksh ", " \-\-pdksh ", " \-\-sh ", " \-\-posix ", " \-\-dash " and " \-\-ash
(those install the Bourne option-letter table), and
.B \-\-no\-rcs
under
.BR \-\-zsh " and " \-\-csh .
Use
.B \-\-no\-rcs
to suppress startup files in any mode.
.TP
.B $PS1
takes bash's backslash escapes under
.BR \-\-bash ,
zsh's
.B %
escapes under
.BR \-\-zsh ,
and is literal text elsewhere.
.TP
.B Terminal sequences
Bracketed paste is written under
.BR \-\-bash " and " \-\-zsh " only; the OSC 133 shell-integration marker and zsh's partial-line " %
marker (PROMPT_SP) are written by native zshrs only. Every drop-in
matches what the shell it stands in for sends.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
In addition to the standard zsh-recognised variables
.RB ( PATH ,
.BR FPATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR UID ,
.BR EUID ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TERM ,
.BR COLUMNS ,
.BR LINES ,
.BR ZDOTDIR ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR MANPATH ,
.BR PS1 ,
.BR PROMPT ,
.BR RPROMPT ),
zshrs reads:
.TP
.B ZSHRS_HOME
State root for caches, databases, logs and the daemon socket (default
.IR $HOME/.zshrs ).
.TP
.B ZSHRS_CACHE
Set to \fB0\fR to disable the caches and the daemon.
.TP
.B ZSHRS_LOG
.BR tracing -style
filter for the structured log written to
.IR $HOME/.zshrs/zshrs.log
.RB "(default " info ).
.TP
.B ZSHRS_LONG_CMD_PRE_THRESHOLD
Seconds before a long-running command warning fires.
.TP
.BR ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ", " OPENAI_API_KEY ", " GOOGLE_API_KEY ", " GEMINI_API_KEY
Provider keys for the
.B ai
builtin, which calls a language model from inside the shell process and
streams the answer or assigns it with
.BR \-v / \-a .
Defaults live in
.B [ai]
in
.IR ~/.zshrs/zshrs.toml ;
.B ai \-h
lists the flags and
.BR zshrsall (1)
documents them.
.TP
.BR ZSHRS_AI_BASE_URL ", " ZSHRS_AI_LOCAL_KEY ", " OLLAMA_HOST
Endpoints (and key) for
.BR "ai \-P local" " and " "ai \-P ollama" .
.TP
.B ZSHRS_AI_MODE
.B mock-only
makes an
.B ai
prompt that no
.B \-M
mock matches an error instead of a live call.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.zshrs/
Cache root (override with
.BR $ZSHRS_HOME ).
Contains
.B scripts.rkyv
(compiled script chunks, keyed by path + mtime),
.B autoloads.rkyv
(one compiled definition program per autoloaded function, keyed by name
and stamped with the definition file's mtime + length),
.I images/
(the daemon's canonical-state and bytecode shards) with
.B index.rkyv
above it,
.BR catalog.db ,
.B compsys.db
and
.B history.db
(read-only SQL mirrors for
.B dbview
and ad-hoc queries \(em never consulted for cache hit/miss),
.B daemon.sock
(when the daemon is running), and
.BR zshrs.log .
The shell's own history is the flat
.B zshrs_history
text file plus its FTS5 index
.BR zshrs_history.db :
a WAL database written by a background thread per shell, so the prompt
never waits on it when many shells share the file.
.TP
.I ~/.zshrs/zshrs-daemon.toml
Optional daemon configuration. See
.BR zd (1).
.TP
.I /etc/zshenv
,
.I ~/.zshenv
,
.I ~/.zprofile
,
.I ~/.zshrc
,
.I ~/.zlogin
,
.I ~/.zlogout
Standard zsh startup files (see
.BR "STARTUP FILES" ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B zshrs
exits with the status of the last command executed, or with the value
of
.B $?
at the time of an
.B exit
builtin call. Non-existent commands exit with status 127; permission
errors with 126.
